Setup. Consider a discrete system of n unit point charges located at distinct points x_1, ..., x_n in the complex plane (or on the real line), interacting via a logarithmic Coulomb potential and possibly subject to an external field generated by a function φ. The associated discrete energy is

  E(x_1, ..., x_n) = -∑_{i<j} log |x_i - x_j| + ∑_i φ(x_i).

A configuration {x_1, ..., x_n} is called:
 (i) a global minimizer if it attains the infimum of E over all admissible configurations;
 (ii) a local minimizer if it attains the infimum of E in some neighborhood;
 (iii) a saddle point (unstable equilibrium) if it is a critical point of E (i.e., ∇E = 0) that is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum;
 (iv) more generally, a stationary point of E.

For many classical families of polynomials (e.g., Jacobi, Laguerre, Hermite, and general orthogonal polynomials with respect to suitable weights), the zeros of the n-th polynomial coincide with the global minimizer of an appropriate discrete energy E. 

Problem. Determine, beyond the global-minimum case, which polynomial families have zero sets that correspond to other types of equilibria of an electrostatic energy functional E—specifically local (non-global) minima, saddle points, or more general stationary configurations that are not minima. In other words, give a systematic characterization (or constructions and examples) of polynomial families whose zeros realize critical points of E that are not global minima, and describe how the type of equilibrium (stable vs. unstable, local vs. global) is reflected in analytic properties of the polynomials (such as orthogonality, differential equations satisfied, or weight regularity).

